          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "In Australian Rules Football, a goal is worth six points and a behind is worth one point. For example, if a team scores four goals and eight behinds, their total score is",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p14-q13-example",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "center",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "(4\\times6)+8=24+8=32.",
              "display": true
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p14-q13-task",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "By a coincidence, the total score in this case is also equal to the number of goals multiplied by the number of behinds. How many other such combinations of goals and behinds can you find?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Four other combinations: $(g,b)=(0,0),\\ (7,7),\\ (3,9),\\ (2,12)$, where $g$ counts goals and $b$ counts behinds.\n\nComplete the product $(g-1)(b-6)=6$ and use integer factor pairs.",
      "worked": "Let $g$ be the number of goals and $b$ the number of behinds, both non-negative integers.\n$$\\begin{align*}gb&=6g+b\\\\gb-6g-b&=0\\\\gb-6g-b+6&=6\\\\g(b-6)-(b-6)&=6\\\\(g-1)(b-6)&=6.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe positive ordered factor pairs $(1,6),(2,3),(3,2),(6,1)$ give $(g,b)=(2,12),(3,9),(4,8),(7,7)$. The negative pair $(-1,-6)$ gives $(0,0)$; other negative pairs give a negative number of goals.\nExcluding the supplied $(4,8)$ example leaves four other combinations:\n$$(g,b)=(0,0),\\ (7,7),\\ (3,9),\\ (2,12).$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 100
  },
